<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=babylonian-astronomy">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babylonian astronomy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as astronomy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Babylonian astronomy refers to the systematic observation and mathematical prediction of celestial phenomena developed in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, flourishing under Chaldean patronage. Its records and methods influenced Greek astronomy after Alexander's conquests. The broader astronomical tradition was later continued by scholars in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, at observatories under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>, and elsewhere.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy.jpg" alt="Babylonian astronomy" width="320" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Linguica at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babylonian_tablet_recording_Halley%27s_comet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Babylonian astronomy was the study and recording of celestial objects in the early history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. It used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sexagesimal">sexagesimal</a> numeral system, based on 60, which simplified the calculation and recording of very great and very small numbers. Among its earliest documents are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrolabe">astrolabes</a>, lists of thirty-six stars tied to the months of the year, generally thought to have been written between 1800 and 1100 BCE. The stars are believed to come from the traditions of three Mesopotamian regions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> and Amurru, which were combined during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>. MUL.APIN, two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets, catalogues stars and constellations. In the 8th and 7th centuries BCE Babylonian astronomers developed a new empirical approach, which some modern scholars have called a scientific revolution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Only fragments survive, mostly clay tablets with astronomical diaries, ephemerides and procedure texts, so knowledge of the planetary theory is incomplete. The Venus tablet of Ammisaduqa, a 7th-century BCE copy of a list of observations that probably dates as early as the second millennium BCE, is the oldest surviving planetary text. Mesopotamians believed that the gods sent omens, most often readable in the sky, and that the events they foretold could be avoided; eclipses were the most dangerous. The Enuma Anu Enlil, from the 7th century BCE, lists omens and the celestial phenomena they relate to.</p><p>The calendar was lunar, with a thirteenth month added to match the growing season. Naburimannu, a priest of the moon god, is the first documented Babylonian astronomer and is credited with tables for the Moon and eclipses.</p><p>Chaldean astronomers of the Neo-Babylonian, Achaemenid, Seleucid and Parthian periods used the diaries to observe the 18-year Saros cycle of lunar eclipses. They noted that the Sun's motion along the ecliptic was not uniform, without knowing why. Their ephemerides rested on arithmetical procedures, but tablets in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> dated between 350 and 50 BCE show that they sometimes used geometrical methods to describe the motion of Jupiter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a>, born in 190 BCE, supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristarchus-of-samos">Aristarchus of Samos</a>'s heliocentric model; he is the only one of the Chaldean astronomers whose planetary model survives, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> lists him among four influential astronomers of Seleuceia on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. In 1900 Franz Xaver Kugler showed that the Moon's periods Ptolemy attributed to Hipparchus had already been used in Babylonian ephemerides, the texts called System B. Herodotus writes that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> learned the gnomon and the division of the day into two halves of twelve from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>. The surviving fragments have been described as the first successful attempt at a refined mathematical description of astronomical phenomena, on which later astronomy in the Hellenistic world, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the West depends.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Babylonian astronomy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Babylonian astronomy</b> and mathematics flourished in this period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Babylonian astronomy</b>, recorded in Akkadian, passed to the Greeks: Greek astronomers used Babylonian observations and methods, and the zodiac of twelve signs, with its division into degrees, is a Babylonian creation.</blockquote><a href="/language/akkadian">Akkadian · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1800 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Babylonia</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The article also gives earlier or vaguer dates, none presented as secure: lunar omens in 'reports from Nineveh and Babylon, circa 2500-670 B.C.', Venus observations that 'probably' go back to the second millennium BC but survive in a 7th-century BC copy (the Venus tablet of Ammisaduqa), and the Omen Compendia, composed from the beginning of the second millennium onwards.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform<span>c. 3300 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sexagesimal">Base-60 numbers<span>3rd millennium BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sundial">Sundial<span>c. 1500 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/heliocentrism">Heliocentrism<span>c. 270 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/antikythera-mechanism">Antikythera mechanism<span>2nd century BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/trigonometry">Trigonometry<span>140 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/astrolabe">Astrolabe<span>4th century CE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg-observatory">Ulugh Beg Observatory<span>1420s</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=babylonian-astronomy">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
